  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The authors explicitly distinguish their marginal-to-conditional learning transition from grokking’s memorization-to-generalization transition, and caution that the connection to language-model asymmetry is a structural analogy, not demonstrated equivalence. Their experiments concern the distinct transition, not the claim versions’ train/test generalization behavior.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports delayed conditional learning on a synthetic selector task, but does not report training accuracy saturating while test accuracy remains at chance, or a train/test split. Its loss plateau and later drop therefore do not establish algorithmic grokking as defined in V1. The reported selector-circuit, optimizer, and dataset-size results concern this distinct task. Results cited from prior grokking work are not recorded as rows.",
    "test_reason": "The paper studies a training-loss plateau at log K followed by a sharp drop on a synthetic selector task. The authors call this related to but distinct from grokking: marginal-to-conditional, not memorization-to-generalization. There is no train/test split, no held-out accuracy and no overfitting phase, so none of V1–V7 is measured. Grokking is cited only as motivation. T6 fits better than the reader's T5: the paper makes no argument that any grokking version is wrong or needs restating.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The plateau height is set by log K and its duration by D. The authors add that gradient noise stabilizes the plateau, that a selector-routing head (L0H3) forms during it before the loss drops, and that the transition is a saddle escape. They frame all of this as distinct from grokking and only structurally analogous to language-model asymmetries.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "A synthetic surjective-map selector task measures delayed marginal-to-conditional prediction, not memorization followed by generalization on a train/test split. The paper is not a replication of either original source.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Waiting time varies with dataset size, batch size, learning rate, and label noise. The authors report no K effect at fixed D. Label noise also degrades the conditional signal, so its effect is confounded.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On the selector task, training loss plateaus at log K (ratio 1.01±0.02), then drops sharply. Waiting time scales as τ∝D^1.19, with no K effect at fixed D.",
        "proposed_claim": "Gradient-trained transformers learn the marginal before the conditional; the plateau's length is set by dataset size.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r5",
          "r6",
          "r7",
          "r8",
          "r9",
          "r10",
          "r11",
          "r12",
          "r13",
          "r14"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "More gradient noise (higher learning rate, smaller batch) lengthens the plateau: 3.6× in tokens across learning rates, 1.8× across batch sizes. Label-noise effects are confounded with a harder task.",
        "proposed_claim": "SGD noise stabilizes the marginal saddle solution (entropic stabilization) rather than helping escape.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r15",
          "r16",
          "r17",
          "r18",
          "r19",
          "r20",
          "r21",
          "r22",
          "r23",
          "r24"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "The transition is collective across groups: 0% of groups solved at τ/2, 87–99% at 1.5τ. A linear network never escapes. The z-shuffle gap onset leads the loss drop by about 0.52τ (no row records this).",
        "proposed_claim": "An internal selector-routing circuit forms during the plateau and precedes a collective transition, analogous to hidden progress measures.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r25",
          "r26",
          "r27",
          "r28",
          "r29"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "y",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/mihirrs/synass-lens",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "The principal sweeps are generally single-seed (seed 42); the paper reports substantial seed variability, with CV 22–71% in available multi-seed data. The main dataset-size sweep spans one decade and the fixed-D control uses two D values. Only one model scale and AdamW are used. The paper itself notes that label-noise results confound noise effects with a harder conditional task, and that the noise-curvature account needs more direct tests. The selector-task results do not measure train/test generalization.",
    "rows_left_out": "No reported results central to delayed marginal-to-conditional learning were omitted. Results on directional asymmetry, hierarchical variants, head ablations, Hessian geometry, selector-length variants, and gradient dissipation were not included because they do not measure the claim versions’ grokking behavior and are secondary to the core transition and timing findings recorded here.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No train/test accuracy; the plateau is in training loss, not test accuracy after training saturation. The waiting time does grow with D (r2), but that is the opposite direction to V1's shrinking training set."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No Fourier or generalizing circuit and no memorization cleanup. The z-shuffle gap leading the loss drop is an analog on a different construct."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No non-algorithmic data, and no initialization-scale or weight-norm manipulation."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No LLM pretraining or fine-tuning."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No weight-decay manipulation, critical dataset size or ungrokking."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No comparison of algorithms across runs."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "Learning-rate and batch-size effects are on the selector-task plateau. No test of adaptive-optimizer instability or softmax numerics as a cause of grokking."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The reader's 'discusses' is fine, but T5 overstates engagement; T6 is better. The z-shuffle lead (Fig 3a, §4) is an analog of V2 progress measures on a different construct and is left out of the rows, which is acceptable. All sweeps are single-seed (seed 42)."
  },
